tbl1 = Table("Which one fits your situation", ["If your project is...", "Lean toward"], [["ML, data pipelines, AI features", "Python"],["Realtime: chat, sockets, live feeds", "Node"],["Full-stack with one language", "Node"],["Large team needing strict structure", "Python with Django"],["Tight CPU-bound number crunching", "Neither alone — add Go or Rust"],["Hiring quickly from a broad pool", "Python"]])
:::

My recommendation: pick **Python** if your product is data-, ML- or AI-heavy, or you value readability and a batteries-included framework like Django. Pick **Node/JavaScript** if you're building I/O-heavy APIs, anything realtime, or you want one language across the whole stack with a shared team.

The real tiebreaker is your team's fluency — either stack will comfortably serve most backends, and swapping later is a rewrite, not a refactor.

Both are excellent relational databases; the honest answer is "it depends on your workload." Here's the short version:

:::intelligent-ui{v="1"}
root = Comparison("PostgreSQL vs MySQL", [pg, my])
pg = {name:"PostgreSQL", attributes:["Strongest SQL standard compliance, richest type system","JSONB, arrays, ranges, geospatial (PostGIS), full-text search","MVCC done right - safe, concurrent DDL, no read locks","Best for complex queries, analytics, data integrity"]}
my = {name:"MySQL / MariaDB", attributes:["Simple, fast for straightforward read-heavy workloads","Huge hosting ecosystem, cheap managed options everywhere","InnoDB is solid; historically looser type/constraint behavior","Best for classic web apps, LAMP stacks, quick CRUD"]}
:::

**My recommendation:** default to **PostgreSQL** for new projects — its richer type system, stricter correctness, and JSONB support mean you rarely outgrow it. Choose **MySQL/MariaDB** if you're on a legacy LAMP stack, need the widest cheap managed hosting, or your workload is simple high-volume read/write CRUD where its operational familiarity matters most.

Practical caveats worth knowing: both scale fine horizontally with read replicas, both are ACID-compliant today, and the performance gap between them is usually smaller than the gap caused by bad schema design or missing indexes.

